#!/bin/sh
# Switch the active orchard-themes theme. Repoints the
# ~/.config/orchard-themes/active symlink to the named theme dir
# and signals every app that supports runtime reload to pick up the
# change. Open ghostty windows update in place.
#
# Usage:
#   set-theme                  list available themes + show current
#   set-theme <name>           switch to <name>
#
# Adding a new app to the theme system: drop a partial named
# <app>.<ext> under each theme dir, point the app's main config at
# ~/.config/orchard-themes/active/<app>.<ext>, and add the app's
# reload command at the bottom of this script (or no-op if the app
# auto-watches its config file).
set -eu

themes_dir=$HOME/.config/orchard-themes
target=${1:-}

if [ -z "$target" ]; then
    echo "Available themes:"
    for d in "$themes_dir"/*/; do
        [ -d "$d" ] || continue
        name=$(basename "$d")
        echo "  $name"
    done
    echo
    if [ -L "$themes_dir/active" ]; then
        echo "Current: $(readlink "$themes_dir/active")"
    else
        echo "Current: (no active theme set)"
    fi
    exit 0
fi

if [ ! -d "$themes_dir/$target" ]; then
    echo "set-theme: '$target' not found under $themes_dir" >&2
    echo "Available:" >&2
    for d in "$themes_dir"/*/; do
        [ -d "$d" ] || continue
        echo "  $(basename "$d")" >&2
    done
    exit 1
fi

# Atomic-ish swap. `ln -sfn` replaces the symlink in place; the
# rename window is small enough that no consumer of the path sees a
# half-state.
ln -sfn "$target" "$themes_dir/active"

# Ghostty reloads on SIGUSR2 specifically. Sending any other signal
# crashes it, so the explicit -SIGUSR2 + -x are both load-bearing.
pkill -SIGUSR2 -x ghostty 2>/dev/null || true

# Waybar reloads its CSS on SIGUSR2 too. The main style.css imports
# ~/.config/orchard-themes/active/waybar.css; once we've repointed
# `active`, the reload picks up the new theme's colors.
pkill -SIGUSR2 -x waybar 2>/dev/null || true

# Mako pulls colors from ~/.config/orchard-themes/active/mako via
# its main config's `include` directive. `makoctl reload` re-reads
# (and runs the include again), so the next notification gets the
# new theme. Existing displayed toasts don't repaint, but those
# expire on their own.
makoctl reload 2>/dev/null || true

# Chromium picks up theme via /etc/chromium/policies/managed/
# orchard-theme.json, a symlink (set up by configure-chromium-theme.sh)
# pointing at this theme dir's chromium.json. Re-reading policy is
# triggered by --refresh-platform-policy on a no-window chromium
# launch — it pushes the change to existing windows live without
# spawning a new one.
if pgrep -x chromium >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    chromium --refresh-platform-policy --no-startup-window </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 &
fi

# Wallpaper. swaybg renders the desktop background as a wlr-layer-
# shell surface; it has no IPC to swap images, so we kill the
# running instance and start a fresh one pointed at the active
# theme's wallpaper. The glob handles whatever extension a theme
# happened to use (.png / .jpg / .webp). If a theme has no
# wallpaper.<ext>, we leave whatever's currently displayed alone.
# `setsid` detaches swaybg from this script's session so it
# survives the script exit.
wallpaper=$(ls "$themes_dir/active/wallpaper".* 2>/dev/null | head -n1)
if [ -n "$wallpaper" ] && [ -f "$wallpaper" ]; then
    pkill -x swaybg 2>/dev/null || true
    setsid swaybg -i "$wallpaper" -m fill </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 &
fi

# Tell every running nvim instance to re-source the active theme
# partial. Each nvim listens on a socket at $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/nvim.<pid>.0
# by default. We use --remote-expr (not --remote-send) for two reasons:
#
#   1. --remote-send interprets the input through nvim's keymap system,
#      so any <C-\> we'd send as a mode-exit gets eaten by vim-tmux-
#      navigator and bounces our tmux focus to the previous pane.
#   2. --remote-expr is a direct expression eval — no keystrokes, no
#      mode juggling. We just call execute() with the colon command.
#
# stdio is redirected to /dev/null so the briefly-spawned nvim client
# can't query terminal capabilities (would leak ^[[?997;1n into the
# shell's input buffer) or echo the empty execute() result.
runtime_dir=${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/run/user/$(id -u)}
for sock in "$runtime_dir"/nvim.*.0; do
    [ -S "$sock" ] || continue
    nvim --server "$sock" --remote-expr \
        "execute('luafile ~/.config/orchard-themes/active/nvim.lua')" \
        </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
done

notify-send "Theme" "Switched to $target"
